the history of German American trade  relations goes back to the late 17th  century Germans began emigrating to what  would later become the United States in  the hope of a more prosperous future in  1683 they founded Germantown today a  suburb of Philadelphia they largely  worked in brewing publishing and banking  in 1785 the then newly born United  States signed a treaty of amity and  commerce with the kingdom of prussia it  oversaw the customs free export of  american tobacco rice and whale oil and  from prussia linen porcelain and iron  increasing unemployment coupled with  famines and political upheavals prompted  a second wave of German immigration  across the Atlantic providing a huge  fresh pool of labor for American farms  and factories they helped boost the  economy of their new home by 1870 one in  seven Americans had German roots an  American industry boomed German  immigrants were responsible for a number  of its future best-selling exports  Levi's Strauss's new business in San  Francisco marked the birth of a street  clothing Empire levi's jeans in the very  same year Heinrich stein vague founded  the piano making company that would  become the world-famous Steinway &amp; Sons  the first part of the 20th century saw  freeze and relations and trade between  the two nations due to the First World  War the later global financial crisis  saw the u.s. stop its loans to Europe in  1934 Nazi Germany terminated a trade  agreement with the US after the Second  World War when West Berlin was blockaded  by the Soviets the city survived thanks  to an american-led airlift and in the  1950s loans and raw materials from the  US helped West Germany undergo its  economic miracle then bilateral trade  really took off again in 1971 McDonald's  opened its first branch in West Germany  in Munich and in 1992 BMW opened its  first factory in the US the partnership  between reunified Germany and the u.s.  continued to intensify and in 2015 the  US overtook France to become Germany's  number one trading partner
 

